<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sadiq Muhammad Khan V</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a>–<a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sadiq Muhammad Khan Abbasi V</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sadiq Muhammad Khan V was the last ruling nawab of Bahawalpur, succeeding as a child in 1907 and reigning until the state merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a> in 1955. In October 1947 he acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and gave the new state generous financial help. His reign saw the irrigation works of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> Valley Project, and he founded Sadiq Public School in Bahawalpur in 1953.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sadiq-muhammad-khan-v.jpg" alt="Sadiq Muhammad Khan V" width="225" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of Bahawalpur · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sadeq_Mohammad_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born at Derawar on 29 September 1904, the only son and heir of Bahawal Khan V. His father died near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> on 15 February 1907, on the way home from a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, and the two-year-old succeeded him. A Council of Regency of Indian officials under Sir Rahim Bakhsh governed for him. He was educated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aitchison-college">Aitchison College</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, fought in the Third Afghan War in 1919 at the age of 15, was knighted in 1922 and received full powers from the Viceroy, Lord Reading, on 8 March 1924.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He also served as an officer of the British Indian Army, rising from lieutenant in 1921 to major general in 1946 and commanding troops in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. He sat in the Chamber of Princes from 1933 and on the Indian Defence Council from 1940. By 1947 Bahawalpur had a cabinet headed by a prime minister, a High Court, a trained police force, an army with officers trained at Dehra Doon and its own bank, the Bank of Bahawalpur. Education was free up to A level, with state scholarships for higher study.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Pandit Nehru</a> tried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to persuade him to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, but he chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, and Bahawalpur was the first princely state to accede to it. He gave the new government seventy million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>, paid a month's salaries of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> from the state treasury and set up a fund for the tens of thousands of Muslim refugees arriving from India. In 1953 he represented Pakistan at the coronation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> and laid the foundation stone of Sadiq Public School, which opened in January 1954.</p><p>Under his 1955 agreement with Governor-General Malik Ghulam Muhammad, Bahawalpur joined the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a> on 14 October 1955, and he kept his titles and precedence with a privy purse of 32 lakhs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> a year. The same year he was promoted to general in the Pakistan Army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 24 May 1966, after 59 years as Nawab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a>, and was buried in his family's ancestral graveyard at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derawar-fort">Derawar Fort</a>. The Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> recognised his eldest son, Muhammad Abbas Khan Abbasi, as his successor to the title; another son, Saeed-ur-Rashid Abbasi, became a federal minister. He also left private property to the University of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, King Edward Medical College and the mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aitchison-college">Aitchison College</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sadiq Muhammad Khan Abbasi V” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sadiq Muhammad Khan V</b> acceded to Pakistan in October 1947 and gave the new state generous financial support.</blockquote><a href="/state/bahawalpur-state">Bahawalpur State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bahawalpur, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.4,71.67&amp;year=1944" aria-label="Bahawalpur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6417 1089 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sadiq Muhammad Khan V"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6867 1359h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6867 1359h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.4,71.67&amp;year=1944">What was here in 1944? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
